The first attribute that allows you to assign a value to a variable in HTML is the "value" attribute. This attribute is used in various HTML elements like input elements to assign a value to a particular attribute.

The second attribute that lets you display the value of a variable on your HTML page is the "innerHTML" attribute. This attribute is used to set the content of a particular element in HTML.

When you put these two attributes together in your HTML code, you can assign a value to a variable and display it on the page. Here's an example: 

```html
<input type="text" value="Hello World" id="myVariable">
<p id="myElement"></p>

<script>
document.getElementById("myVariable").onchange = function() {
    document.getElementById("myElement").innerHTML = this.value;
};
</script>
```

In this example, we have an input element with an ID of "myVariable" and a "value" attribute that is initially set to "Hello World". We also have a paragraph element with an ID of "myElement". We then add a script tag that uses the getElementById function to access both elements. When the value of the "myVariable" input element changes, we set the "innerHTML" attribute of the "myElement" paragraph element to the new value. This therefore displays the value of the variable on the page.

To create a singleton API client for React using Axios, you can follow the steps below:

Step 1: Import Axios and create a new instance of it in a separate file called `ApiClient.js`.
```javascript
import axios from 'axios';

const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://your-api-url.com',
});

export default api;
```
In this example, we are setting the base URL of the API client to "https://your-api-url.com". You can update this with the actual URL of the API you want to consume.

Step 2: In your React component, import the `ApiClient` and use it to make API calls.
```javascript
import api from './ApiClient';

export default function App() {
  const getData = async () => {
    const response = await api.get('/api/data');
    console.log(response.data);
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={getData}>Get Data</button>
  );
}
```
In this example, we are importing the `ApiClient` and using it to make a GET request to the "/api/data" endpoint.

By creating a singleton instance of the Axios client, you can ensure that all API calls are being made using the same instance, and any configurations or interceptors that you set up will be applied to all requests.

