Sure, here's a simple implementation of the merge sort algorithm in C#. Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that divides the input array into smaller sub-arrays, call them left and right. It then recursively sorts both sub-arrays and then merges the sorted sub-arrays.

Here is the C# code:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;

class MergeSort 
{
    private static void Merge(int[] array, int left, int mid, int right) 
    {
        int n1 = mid - left + 1; 
        int n2 = right - mid;  
        int[] leftArray = new int[n1]; 
        int[] rightArray = new int[n2]; 

        for (int i = 0; i < n1; i++) 
            leftArray[i] = array[left + i]; 
        for (int j = 0; j < n2; j++) 
            rightArray[j] = array[mid + 1+ j]; 

        int i = 0, j = 0, k = left; 
        while ((i < n1) && (j < n2)) {
            if (leftArray[i] <= rightArray[j]) {
                array[k] = leftArray[i]; 
                i++; 
            } else {
                array[k] = rightArray[j]; 
                j++; 
            }
            k++; 
        }

        while (i < n1) {
            array[k] = leftArray[i]; 
            i++; 
            k++; 
        }

        while (j < n2) {
            array[k] = rightArray[j]; 
            j++; 
            k++; 
        }
    }

    private static void MergeSort(int[] array, int left, int right) 
{
        if (left < right) {
            int mid = left + (right - left) / 2; 

            MergeSort(array, left, mid);
            MergeSort(array, mid + 1, right); 

            Merge(array, left, mid, right);
        }
    }

    public static void Sort(int[] array) 
    {
        MergeSort(array, 0, array.Length - 1);
    }
}

class Program 
{
    static void Main(string[] args) 
    {
        int[] array = { 120, 14, 24, 75, 33, 25, 321, 2, 22, 11, 144 };
        MergeSort.Sort(array);

        Console.WriteLine("Sorted array: ");
        foreach (var item in array)
            Console.Write(" " + item);
    }
}
```

Here's how the code works:
- The `Merge` method combines sorted left and right sub-arrays into one sorted array.
- The `MergeSort` method is a recursive method that divides the input array into smaller sub-arrays and sorts them with the help of the `Merge` method.
- The `Sort` method in the `MergeSort` class sorts the entire array by calling the `MergeSort` method with the start and end index of the entire array.
- In the `Main` method, we call the `Sort` method to sort an array.
- Finally, we print the sorted array to the console.

The time complexity of merge sort is O(n log n), where n is the number of elements in the array. This makes it one of the most efficient sorting algorithms for large data sets.

